{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfAG9F2jeytCKZ1cTFszd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I submitted index 25 (Sb fraction 0.926) as the best composition, but the choice is close to arbitrary. None of the five compositions I measured showed a real photoresponse.\n\n| Index | Sb fraction | EQE (%) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 0.237 | 0.00063 |\n| 9 | 0.503 | 0.00095 |\n| 13 | 0.643 | 0.00092 |\n| 14 | 0.758 | -0.00149 |\n| 25 | 0.926 | 0.00113 |\n\nAll five values are about zero, and one is negative, so the differences are almost certainly noise. The likely cause is that zinc–antimony oxides absorb poorly at 2.4 eV. For example, ZnSb₂O₆ has a band gap of about 3.3 eV, well above the light energy used here. That is from general knowledge; I didn't confirm it with any tool.\n\nI started at 0.64 and 0.76, near the ZnSb₂O₆ composition, expecting the best result there. When both came back flat I measured the two ends of the line, and I spent the last measurement near the middle (0.50), close to what the Gaussian-process model suggested.\n\nI used none of the five XRD measurements, so which crystal phases are present is unconfirmed. Of the 26 candidates, 21 were never measured. A composition with a real signal could be among them, but the flat result at all five spread-out points makes that unlikely."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":541,"cache_read_input_tokens":6721,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":541},"output_tokens":476,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64c0c757-6529-4d43-890e-6c7bbb79fae7","uuid":"1ab8cd9d-47f5-4b2f-889e-72ea0480b489","timestamp":"2026-09-18T04:19:25.135Z","request_id":"req_011CfAG9EbvoKWuaq5zTqM5E"}
